FINDING 1 (plan-premise error, critical): ExUnit.Filters.eval/4 (Elixir 1.18.4,
/home/mei/.nix-profile/lib/elixir/lib/ex_unit/lib/ex_unit/filters.ex) gives an include match
ABSOLUTE priority: cond first clause "Enum.any?(include, &has_tag(&1, tags, collection)) ->
maybe_skipped(...)" returns :ok before the exclude is ever consulted. mix test.full passes
--include slow --include full_catalog ... (slow_tags) and the two monsters carry @tag :full_catalog
+ @tag :slow + @tag :nightly, so they match test.full's include and RUN despite the helper's
exclude: [:nightly]. The plan's premise "test.full excludes nightly via the helper" is false; the
task's membership proof "test.full output does NOT contain the two nightly names" is therefore
unreachable with the pinned 2-file wiring (no ExUnit wiring can beat include-wins). Consequence:
deep.yml:293's existing "--exclude nightly" on the full-suite job is likewise ineffective for these
monsters (same eval path) — the "30-min corpus monsters" comment at deep.yml:205-206 describes an
intent the flag never delivered. RECOMMENDED AMENDMENT (orchestrator decision, outside this todo's
2-file scope): drop @tag :full_catalog and @tag :slow from the two monsters (real_catalog_importer
_test.exs:158-161 and importer_provider_test.exs:205-208), keeping @tag :nightly + timeout — their
own comment already claims "Excluded from default mix test.full via @tag :nightly". Then test.full's
include no longer matches them and the helper exclude applies. Membership for bare mix test,
test.fast, and --only nightly is otherwise fully proven.
